apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: nginx-pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: nginx-container
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
    - containerPort: 80
      hostPort: 8080

What happens here:

The container listens on port 80.
The host machine maps port 8080 to the container’s port 80.
You can access nginx via http://<host-ip>:8080.

In a multi-node Kubernetes cluster (like your example with 5 worker nodes), if you define a Pod with hostPort: 8080, here's what happens:
✅ What hostPort Does in a Multi-Node Cluster

The Pod runs on one specific node (e.g., worker-node-1) with ip address : 192.168.1.0
The nginx container listens on port 80.
The host machine (worker-node-1) with ip address : 192.168.1.0 maps its port 8080 to the container's port 80. 
https://192.168.1.0:8080  ---> access ngix-container.
The hostPort binds the Pod to a specific node.

kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o wide --> Get the IP address of Workernod1 

 

